<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hungarian Diet</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hungarian Diet was the parliament of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a> it governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s internal affairs within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Dual Monarchy</a>, while sharing foreign policy, defence and related finance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> elite, it was a central institution in the governance of the Austro-Hungarian Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hungarian-diet.jpg" alt="Hungarian Diet" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Assembly_of_Hungary_(1830).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Some researchers trace national assemblies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to the reigns of King Ladislaus I and King Coloman in the 11th century, when church and lay dignitaries met on important occasions. The word parlamentum first appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1222">Golden Bull of 1222</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II</a>, which confirmed the rights of the lesser nobles, the royal servants, against the crown and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>; their practice of presenting grievances to the king grew into the Diet. In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> documents it was usually the Congregatio Generalis, while Diéta, taken from the German imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>, spread from the Jagiellonian period, alongside the Hungarian országgyűlés.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>An institutional parliament emerged in the 14th and 15th centuries, though under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis the Great</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> it often did little more than approve royal decisions. It regained much of its power under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a>. In 1492 the Diet limited the serfs' freedom of movement, and after the peasant rising under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyorgy-dozsa">György Dózsa</a> in 1514 it condemned them to eternal bondage. From the 13th century law-making was a joint right of the king and the Diet, and after the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a> died out the Diet often elected the king. In 1608 it divided into a House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">Magnates</a> and a House of Representatives.</p><p>The Diet of the Estates ended in 1848. During the revolution an assembly meeting at the Great Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debrecen">Debrecen</a> declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> deposed and elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth">Lajos Kossuth</a> regent-president, before Austrian troops under General Julius Jacob von Haynau suppressed the revolution. Talks after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solferino">Battle of Solferino</a> in 1859 failed, and under the Compromise of 1867 the emperor appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyula-andrassy">Gyula Andrássy</a> minister-president and the national assembly met again on 27 February. From 1902 it sat in the Parliament Building on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">Magnates</a> had no fixed size: it held princes of the royal house, hereditary peers paying at least 3000 florins a year in land tax, church dignitaries, life peers and three delegates of Croatia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>. The House of Representatives had 453 members, 413 elected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and 40 sent by the parliament of Croatia-Slavonia, for five-year terms. Under the Electoral Law of 1874 the franchise rested on property, taxation, profession and ancestral privilege; the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition called it probably the most illiberal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1910, in a kingdom about 54.5% <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a>, 405 of the 413 representatives were of Hungarian descent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diet of Hungary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Dual Monarchy divided sovereignty between the emperor-king and two parliamentary systems, the Austrian Reichsrat in Vienna and the <b>Hungarian Diet</b> in Budapest, each sovereign within its own territory, while joint ministers for foreign affairs, war and the finances supporting them answered to Delegations drawn from both parliaments.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Hungarian Diet</b> at Pressburg, Kossuth called for a responsible government and constitutional rule.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-3">3 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
